WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.770
XML processing can be resource intensive.

2
00:00:02.770 --> 00:00:14.999
The application may spend CPU parsing structure, memory holding nodes, network access resolving resources, disk space storing uploads, and time validating or transforming documents.

3
00:00:14.999 --> 00:00:21.350
Reliability problems can appear even when the document is not accepted as valid business data.

4
00:00:21.350 --> 00:00:29.052
Large documents, deeply nested structures, expensive validation, and memory-heavy loading can all strain services.

5
00:00:29.052 --> 00:00:33.444
That is why size expectations should be part of the XML contract.

6
00:00:33.444 --> 00:00:45.740
The service should know the maximum request body size, maximum upload size, maximum document character count, timeout behavior, and what happens when a document exceeds those limits.

7
00:00:45.740 --> 00:00:47.092
In .

8
00:00:47.092 --> 00:00:57.091
NET, parser settings such as maximum document characters and maximum characters from entities should be reviewed where relevant to the parsing path.

9
00:00:57.091 --> 00:01:07.766
The exact settings depend on the API and workflow, but the principle is consistent: the application should not accept unbounded work from an XML input source.

10
00:01:07.766 --> 00:01:10.806
Controls should exist at more than one layer.

11
00:01:10.806 --> 00:01:21.143
Web servers, reverse proxies, gateways, upload handlers, queues, storage policies, parsers, validation code, and background workers may each need limits.

12
00:01:21.143 --> 00:01:31.075
Cancellation and timeout handling are also important so the application can stop work that no longer has a valid caller or useful business outcome.

13
00:01:31.075 --> 00:01:40.669
Streaming can help because the application can process incrementally and reject early, but streaming is not a substitute for a limit strategy.

14
00:01:40.669 --> 00:01:48.844
If the workflow eventually builds an in-memory object graph or performs validation, those later stages need controls too.

15
00:01:48.844 --> 00:01:54.993
The whole XML path should be reviewed from arrival to storage to parsing to downstream use.

16
00:01:54.993 --> 00:01:58.101
Operational monitoring completes the baseline.

17
00:01:58.101 --> 00:02:07.627
Track parsing failures, oversized documents, validation failures, timeouts, unusual XML volume, and repeated failures by workflow or partner.

18
00:02:07.627 --> 00:02:16.613
These signals help teams detect misconfiguration, broken integrations, and resource pressure before they become production incidents.
